WEBVTT

00:00:11.906 --> 00:00:13.058
What alerts when it breaks.

00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:16.840
When payments break, who finds out,

00:00:17.138 --> 00:00:17.838
and how?

00:00:17.906 --> 00:00:18.973
Alarms do exist.

00:00:19.826 --> 00:00:21.490
The payment service has eight monitors.

00:00:21.881 --> 00:00:23.843
Payments runs its own, with a verdict

00:00:24.014 --> 00:00:24.714
every morning.

00:00:25.038 --> 00:00:27.129
A card-testing watch checks every hour.

00:00:27.683 --> 00:00:29.987
And a daily watch looks for cards left charged.

00:00:33.416 --> 00:00:35.336
But an alarm that fires is not an

00:00:35.378 --> 00:00:36.104
alarm that is read.

00:00:36.786 --> 00:00:38.322
In September, in one market,

00:00:38.877 --> 00:00:40.882
the right alarm fired on the first day,

00:00:41.224 --> 00:00:43.144
naming the right processor and the right

00:00:43.272 --> 00:00:46.130
fault, and again every hour for seven days.

00:00:46.673 --> 00:00:48.209
The fault was found on day seven,

00:00:48.721 --> 00:00:50.470
by a person reading orders by hand.

00:00:53.948 --> 00:00:54.972
Why did nobody hear it?

00:00:55.569 --> 00:00:57.020
The alarm was already red for another

00:00:57.105 --> 00:00:58.855
problem, so its state never changed.

00:00:59.324 --> 00:01:01.628
A hundred and ninety-five alert messages

00:01:01.841 --> 00:01:03.121
landed in that channel that day.

00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:05.340
And the four people in it could not

00:01:05.468 --> 00:01:06.236
pick out the right one.

00:01:09.544 --> 00:01:10.397
It was not a one-off.

00:01:10.824 --> 00:01:12.616
Across sixty-three real faults,

00:01:13.085 --> 00:01:15.048
the middle time before anyone noticed was

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:15.790
seven days.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:17.906
Eleven ran for more than a month.

00:01:18.461 --> 00:01:20.168
And not one was caught because a system

00:01:20.424 --> 00:01:21.124
noticed first.

00:01:24.327 --> 00:01:25.223
There is a second gap.

00:01:25.821 --> 00:01:27.826
Every alarm watched for bad things happening.

00:01:28.594 --> 00:01:30.215
Nothing watched for good things not happening:

00:01:30.898 --> 00:01:33.202
the automatic rebill retry was dead for

00:01:33.245 --> 00:01:35.122
twenty-two nights, about three thousand

00:01:35.335 --> 00:01:37.895
attempts, none charged, and no alarm.

00:01:41.073 --> 00:01:43.078
So our own alarms follow three rules.

00:01:43.718 --> 00:01:45.041
Speak only when something changes.

00:01:45.596 --> 00:01:46.961
Put the verdict in the first line.

00:01:47.345 --> 00:01:49.222
And before anyone says nothing alerted,

00:01:49.606 --> 00:01:50.972
read the alarm history first.

00:01:53.328 --> 00:01:54.395
What alerts when it breaks:

00:01:54.992 --> 00:01:56.955
the alarm has to fire, reach the right

00:01:56.997 --> 00:01:58.549
people, and be read.

